One way to think about this assignment is to “shift” protection from fields with very low current threat (Field_1 and Field_4 both have a threat level of 0.04) toward the field under the highest bird threat (Field_2, with threat 0.14) while still ensuring that Field_3 (threat 0.09) remains safely covered. Originally Field_1 was already fully protected by four drones and Field_4 had two drones en route, but since Field_2 had no drones at all, we can afford to reassign some drones from the “extra‐protection” at low–risk fields.

In this allocation the single remaining drone in Field_1 (Drone_7) will leave Field_1 only partially protected. However, given that its threat level is only 0.04 it is acceptable if birds cause some localized nuisance rather than a full-blown crop loss (they scare around instead of fleeing outright). On the other hand, Field_2 now gets four drones (Drone_2, Drone_5, Drone_6, and Drone_8) which is the full count needed so that when they arrive the birds will be driven off completely. Field_3 already had two drones (Drone_1 and Drone_3) moving in for full protection, and Field_4 retains one drone (Drone_4) even though that field, with only a 0.04 threat level, will be less than fully secured. This trade‐off overall minimizes the likely damage to the crops across the farm.
